<div id="A4.SS8.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS8.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the final layer, <math id="A4.SS8.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{n\_out}=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">n_out</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{n\_out}=1000</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS9" class="ltx_subsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.9 </span>Training</h4>

<div id="A4.SS9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For all methods, we use either the Adam optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma &amp; Ba, 2015</a>)</cite> or the AdamW optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loshchilov &amp; Hutter, 2019</a>)</cite> with default parameters. This means that both optimizer are used with <math id="A4.SS9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=0.9</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{2}=0.999" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.999</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{2}=0.999</annotation></semantics></math>. All parameters are given in the following table:</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.SS9.tab1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="A4.SS9.tab1.1" class="ltx_logical-block">
                        
<div id="A4.SS9.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent ltx_align_center">
